When faces of one family tie on weight, width and slope, Ladybird finds
a face without a cmap subtable of the Windows symbol encoding ahead of
one with it. So for “font-family: Hoefler Text”, it finds Hoefler
Text Regular, not Hoefler Text Ornaments. Run on macOS at weights 300,
400 and 500, Chrome 158, Firefox 151 and WebKit 26.5 all render Hoefler
Text Regular there as well.

Blink’s BestStyleMatchForFamilyNS() takes the first best match in the
order that NSFontManager’s “availableMembersOfFontFamily:” lists.
WebKit’s FontDatabase lists a family’s faces in the order CoreText’s
CTFontDescriptorCreateMatchingFontDescriptors() gives, and its
findClosestFont() picks from those. Both orders put Regular first, and
CoreText puts HoeflerText-Ornaments in its symbolic class. Gecko’s
CoreTextFontList marks faces named Regular, Bold, Italic, Oblique, Bold
Italic and Bold Oblique as standard faces, and prefers those on a tie.

Ladybird doesn’t prefer by style name: For Avenir at weight 400 and
italic, the standard name picks Avenir Oblique, which Firefox renders,
while Chrome and WebKit render Avenir Book Oblique, as Ladybird does.
Among the fonts installed on macOS, Hoefler Text is the only family
whose ties the symbol encoding changes.
